{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788982138,"nonce":"FCnFWI3geddc7dJX9MOkRU7ho/g=","message":"ArraySize and GetTID are done in bfe4155 -- two of the eight.\n\nBoth used the null/load/cont diamond GetType already uses. ArraySize answers 0 and GetTID answers the register's static type kind, matching the interpreter. A select does not work here: it evaluates both arms and the load is what faults.\n\nPerf gate passed on its third attempt, and the first two are worth recording because they were both wrong:\n\n  1. Against baselines from an earlier session -- invalid, different binary vintage.\n  2. On the NUC while rayzor was building on it (load 7.91, two foreign rustc) -- invalid, and caught by the user rather than the harness.\n  3. ABBA of two binaries differing ONLY by these guards, one quiet box, 8 pairs per row, 4 CPUs and unrestricted.\n\nOnly the third is worth anything, and it says no effect: deltablue -5.7% unrestricted against +3.6% at 4 CPUs (a change cannot both help and hurt one row), nbody +2.5%/+0.6% with the guards' MINIMUM below base's, mandelbrot -1.6%/0.0%. Every range overlaps. The earlier deltablue-4cpu scare was that row's own 55-73ms spread.\n\nRemaining six -- EnumIndex, EnumField, SetEnumField, Unref, Setref, ToVirtual -- all dereference for a VALUE rather than a constant, so each needs its own oracle read from the interpreter and its own A/B. Batching them would not say which one moved a number.","files":null}]}